### System information

linux

### Issue description
_(sorry i deviated from the template)_

so im implementing my own gdextension binding for rust, and as part of a some ideas i'm developing, i was using a `CodeEditor` node, and when i called `set_syntax_highlighter`, it segfaulted.
i thought i was doing something wrong, but with other calls involving `RefCounted` it works as expected (or rather the other way around)
i'm not very familiar with cpp so i cannot confirm this, but for example

`Control.add_theme_font_override`: increments the count
`TextEdit.set_syntax_highlighter`: doesnt

and if i look at the arguments of those methods in godot
```cpp
// receives a const Ref<Font> &
void add_theme_font_override(const StringName &p_name, const Ref<Font> &p_font);
```
```cpp
// receives directly Ref<SyntaxHighlighter>
void set_syntax_highlighter(Ref<SyntaxHighlighter> p_syntax_highlighter);
```
so i looked for some other method that would receive a `Ref<T>` and indeed the same happened.

i believe that on the when `PrtArg<Ref<T>>.convert` is called on one of these functions
```cpp
template <typename T, typename R, typename... P, size_t... Is>
void call_with_ptr_args_static_retc_helper(T *p_instance, R (*p_method)(T *, P...), const void **p_args, void *r_ret, IndexSequence<Is...>) {
	PtrToArg<R>::encode(p_method(p_instance, PtrToArg<P>::convert(p_args[Is])...), r_ret);
}
```
comes here
```cpp
_FORCE_INLINE_ static Ref<T> convert(const void *p_ptr) {
		if (p_ptr == nullptr) {
			return Ref<T>();
		}
		// p_ptr points to a RefCounted object
		return Ref<T>(*reinterpret_cast<T *const *>(p_ptr));
	}

// which goes to the ptr constructor
Ref(T *p_from) {
	this->operator=(p_from);
}
// which goes to the operator
void operator=(T *p_from) {
	ref_pointer<true>(p_from);
}
// which goes to ref_pointer
template <bool Init>
_FORCE_INLINE_ void ref_pointer(T *p_refcounted) {
	if (p_refcounted == reference) {
		return;
	}
	// This will go out of scope and get unref'd.
	Ref cleanup_ref;
	cleanup_ref.reference = reference;
	reference = p_refcounted;
	if (reference) {
		if constexpr (Init) {
			if (!reference->init_ref()) {
				reference = nullptr;
			}
		} else {
			if (!reference->reference()) {
				reference = nullptr;
			}
		}
	}
}
```
`Init` is true, and i believe `init_ref` also returns true, so the reference remains valid.
the only `init_ref` i can find on the repo is the one on the `RefCounted` class
```cpp
bool RefCounted::init_ref() {
	if (reference()) {
		if (!is_referenced() && refcount_init.unref()) {
			unreference(); // first referencing is already 1, so compensate for the ref above
		}

		return true;
	} else {
		return false;
	}
}
```
which i dont fully understand `refcount_init` but it does not exit with a different counter

what i believe happens is in the PtrArgs godot get a raw, uncounted ref, when it calls the actual method, if the method expects a `Ref*/&` then the cpp compile creates a it, and then the method by assigning to one of the object prop or some, calls the copy ctor
```cpp
Ref(const Ref &p_from) {
	this->operator=(p_from);
}
// which calls the operator=
template <typename T_Other>
void operator=(const Ref<T_Other> &p_from) {
	ref_pointer<false>(Object::cast_to<T>(p_from.ptr()));
}
```
which calls `ref_pointer` but with `false` this time, simple incrementing the count
